  <ol class="cmp-notes">
    <li>The verifier returns a checked module that gates execution; capability requirements and effect rows resolve first, and VM checkpoints are re-verified on restore rather than trusted. Nothing else here does this. Anthropic's code-execution-with-MCP, Cloudflare Code Mode, CodeAct/smolagents, E2B and WASM tool sandboxes all <em>isolate</em> execution instead of checking it; pre-execution effect verification exists only in research.</li>
    <li><code>agent persist</code> and <code>agent persist attach</code>.</li>
    <li>A daemon mode with named-session reattach exists as an open, unmerged pull request; what ships today is transcript resume.</li>
    <li>The CLI's hub daemon owns session state; the IDE extension resumes transcripts instead.</li>
    <li>Event log plus typed VM checkpoint on disk, re-verified on restore; covered by restart regression tests in the Brain store.</li>
    <li>Survives supervisor self-restart, but falls back to reconstructing from disk on reboot and marks a session failed after 48 hours.</li>
    <li>Not documented either way.</li>
    <li>Designed to, but in-flight tool calls are reported to freeze permanently on server restart.</li>
    <li>Driver, Consultant and Observer.</li>
    <li>Single-writer — “this session is running in another terminal”.</li>
    <li>One live session shared by several clients, but flat access with no role distinction.</li>
    <li>ChatGPT only.</li>
    <li>A Cursor account is required regardless; bring-your-own-key changes only who pays for tokens.</li>
    <li>Consumer login ended 18 June 2026; users were moved to a separate successor CLI.</li>
    <li>ChatGPT Plus, GitHub Copilot and GitLab Duo with zero setup. The Claude Pro/Max plugins were unbundled at 1.3.0 — Anthropic prohibits that use.</li>
    <li>Shells out to a separately installed Claude Code rather than implementing its own login.</li>
    <li>Native for ChatGPT and Gemini; Claude only through a third-party passthrough.</li>
    <li>A configured local profile does not yet guarantee a query is routed locally.</li>
    <li>Built-in <code>ollama</code> and <code>lmstudio</code> provider IDs, dedicated crates, an <code>--oss</code> picker, default ports 11434 and 1234.</li>
    <li>The community pull request was closed unmerged; a fork carries it.</li>
    <li>Accessibility is stated as a project invariant in Finch and is not met: the only click path takes raw screen coordinates. Adjacent work is tracked — <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/310" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#310</a> for accessible file-context selection and <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/453" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#453</a> for semantic GUI addressing — but no screen-reader mode is scoped, so this stays ○ rather than ▣.</li>
    <li><code>--ax-screen-reader</code>, labelled turn prefixes, numbered menus replacing arrow-key navigation, OSC 133 turn markers.</li>
    <li>A documented screen-reader setting, with open regressions against it.</li>
    <li>The screen-reader request was closed as “not planned”.</li>
    <li>An approval boundary only — no Seatbelt, bubblewrap, Landlock or seccomp anywhere in the source today. The mechanism is scoped and tracked: <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/434" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#434</a> decides the per-platform sandbox for confining shell commands, and <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/89" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#89</a> runs model edits in isolated changeset overlays. The mark stays ○ because planned is not shipped.</li>
    <li>PolyForm Noncommercial: source-available, and business use needs a commercial licence.</li>
    <li>Seven provider families in <code>finch setup</code>, plus local and remote-daemon profile types. A subscription credential and an API key never cross-bind.</li>
    <li>Any endpoint via <code>[model_providers.&lt;id&gt;]</code>, but it must speak OpenAI's Responses or Chat Completions wire shape, so a competing vendor's native API needs a shim in front of it.</li>
    <li>Multi-vendor including your own key, but the documentation states all requests route through Cursor's servers for final prompt building.</li>
    <li>Anthropic's gateway documentation states it does not support routing Claude Code to non-Claude models. Bedrock and Vertex are still Claude, on a different cloud.</li>
    <li>Only <code>GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL</code> and <code>GOOGLE_VERTEX_BASE_URL</code> overrides, both Google. OpenAI-compatible support is an open, unshipped request.</li>
    <li>Tracked in <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/320" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#320</a>, investigate Tree-sitter repository maps for context selection.</li>
    <li>The canonical example. Tree-sitter extracts symbol definitions and references, then PageRank over the reference graph ranks them to fit the context budget — structural centrality, not vector similarity.</li>
    <li>An <code>analyze</code> tool returns tree-sitter symbol listings and call graphs, computed fresh per call with no persistence, so it is a tool rather than an index.</li>
    <li>Not verifiable. The CLI documents fast grep and an Explore subagent; whether the editor's separate embedding index applies to the CLI is not stated either way.</li>
    <li>Tracked in <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/135" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#135</a>, finish end-to-end image paste and attachment support.</li>
    <li><code>/add</code>, <code>/paste</code> or a filename argument; explicitly requires a vision-capable model.</li>
    <li>Paste and drag-and-drop in the desktop app, capped at ten images per message, plus an <code>image_read</code> tool agent-side.</li>
    <li>Not verifiable. The CLI documentation is silent on images; open community requests suggest it is unsupported, which is not a primary confirmation.</li>
    <li>A restricted Brain TLS listener for collaboration, opened only when service advertisement is enabled. That is peer attachment, not mirroring a live console to a phone.</li>
    <li>Remote Control connects claude.ai or the mobile apps to a session running on your machine; execution and filesystem access stay local. Distinct from the cloud-executed web product.</li>
    <li>The mobile app pairs by QR code to the <em>desktop app</em> over a relay, with execution local. Not confirmed to reach a session started from a terminal.</li>
    <li><code>opencode serve</code> exposes a local HTTP server another device can drive, optionally network-bound with basic auth. <code>/share</code> is a read-only transcript link instead.</li>
    <li><code>goose roam</code> exists in documentation but ships in no released binary — it requires building from source behind a feature flag, so a reader downloading Goose today does not get it.</li>
    <li>Not verifiable. <code>cline kanban</code> is a local task board; remote access to an already-running session is not documented.</li>
    <li>An open, unimplemented request to expose Goose itself over MCP; it is an MCP client today.</li>
    <li>One undocumented event. If <code>~/.finch/hooks/post-save</code> exists it is spawned with the file path after an edit or write succeeds, fire-and-forget: Finch does not wait for it, read its output, or act on its exit code. It cannot gate or alter an operation the way a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook elsewhere can, and there is no configuration and no other event. Reading Claude Code and Codex hook configurations natively — so existing scripts work unchanged, with gating and diagnostics fed back into the model&rsquo;s next turn — is specified in <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/470" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#470</a>.</li>
    <li>Not verifiable. <code>hooks.json</code> is real and runs user code, but the documentation covers the editor and cloud agents; no CLI reference states that <code>cursor-agent</code> loads them.</li>
    <li>Available in the CLI, SDK and Kanban, and explicitly not in the editor extensions.</li>
    <li>An Open Plugins hooks specification. Its own documentation notes that <code>SubagentStart</code> and <code>Stop</code> are declared but never actually emitted.</li>
    <li><code>--lint-cmd</code> and <code>--test-cmd</code> fire at two fixed points. There is no named-event hook API.</li>
    <li>Only the <code>finch agent</code> autonomous mode stages and commits, using a persona git identity, and only when the task names a repository. The interactive console never does. That mode is an early prototype rather than a supported path, so read this ◐ narrowly.</li>
    <li>Checkpointing and Checkpoints are internal or shadow-repository snapshots; their own documentation states they do not commit to your project's git history, which is why they do not score here.</li>
    <li>Forbidden in the base model instructions — do not commit unless explicitly requested.</li>
    <li>Only the GitHub Actions bot integration commits by itself, from its own wrapper rather than the model. The ordinary CLI does not.</li>
    <li>Default on. Commits every edit with a generated message, and commits pre-existing dirty changes before editing.</li>
    <li><strong>Read this row narrowly.</strong> Every harness here will commit when you tell it to, and instructing a commit after each discrete edit — through <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code> or a prompt — is the ordinary way people work, including on Finch itself. The row records only what a tool does with no instruction at all, which is a difference in default rather than in capability. Codex is ○ because its base instructions actively forbid an uninstructed commit, not because it cannot make one.</li>
    <li>Six critique personas activate on deterministic keyword rules. An item at severity 8 and confidence 7 or above is must-address and blocks convergence, which additionally requires the plan to stabilise within a 15% character delta, over at most three iterations by default. The purpose is to let a cheaper model reach a better answer by constraining how it reasons and iterating before you ever see the result — paid for in tokens. User steering is still open in <a href="https://github.com/darwin-finch/finch/issues/22" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#22</a>.</li>
    <li>The near neighbours, none of which iterate: plan modes in Claude Code, Cursor and Gemini CLI analyse and propose in a single pass; Codex's optional <code>auto_review</code> screens approval requests with a reviewer agent; Aider's architect mode splits proposing from editing across two models. No convergence criterion and nothing that blocks on unresolved critique.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="table-note">These facts drift quickly; if you are reading this long after September 2026, re-check before relying on a row. Two naming notes: OpenCode&rsquo;s repository moved from the <code>sst</code> organisation to <code>anomalyco</code>, and Goose moved from Block to the Linux Foundation&rsquo;s Agentic AI Foundation in April 2026. Aider is included because it remains widely used, though its main branch has been untouched since May 2026.</p>
